<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Tang</h1><p class="st-meta">923 CE – 937 CE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second of the Five Dynasties, founded by the Shatuo Turkic ruler Li Cunxu, who claimed to restore the Tang and briefly reunited much of northern China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, proclaimed himself emperor of a restored Tang in 923 and within months captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a>. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, the old Tang eastern capital, and in 925 conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruling house and much of its army were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>, a brilliant general, alienated his troops by favouring actors and was killed in a mutiny in 926. His adopted brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Li Siyuan (Emperor Mingzong)</a> restored order, and under his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-dao">Feng Dao</a> work began in 932 on the first printed edition of the Confucian classics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Mingzong</a>'s death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-congke">Li Congke</a> seized the throne in 934. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang">Shi Jingtang</a> rebelled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> with Khitan help, promising them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>. Li Congke burned himself to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> as their armies arrived, and Shi Jingtang founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin</a> (936-947).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-tang.jpg" alt="Later Tang" width="320" height="298"><figcaption>Image: 玖巧仔 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%94%E4%BB%A3%E5%90%8E%E5%94%90%EF%BC%88%E7%B9%81%EF%BC%89.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>923 CE – 937 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Cunxu (Emperor Zhuangzong), Li Siyuan (Emperor Mingzong), Li Congke</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Shatuo Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tang (923-937)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=later-tang" aria-label="Later Tang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7578 869 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Tang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7898 1125L7855 1179M7898 1125L7807 1072M7898 1125L7729 1184M7898 1125L7807 1072M7898 1125L7920 1337M7898 1125L7696 1315M7898 1125L7696 1315"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7807 1072h0M7729 1184h0M7807 1072h0M7920 1337h0M7696 1315h0M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7898 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7898 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=930&amp;focus=later-tang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
